import json,pathlib
ROOT=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
r=json.loads((ROOT/'results/los-frontier-enemy-probe.json').read_text());assert len(r['results'])==4
lines=['# Pointing the frontier cue at an adjacent enemy','','Frozen 10M seed-0 LOS/rest/sleep policy. No training or forced-action heuristic. Same 1,024 validation episodes and original 24-enemy, LOS-gated waking, 2,048-decision environment.','','## Intervention','','Only edit the policy input while a legal visible adjacent enemy exists. Target the lowest-HP adjacent enemy, compass-order ties. Enemy, terrain, sleep, player and reward information remain unchanged. The environment and actual frontier computation remain unchanged.','','- **route_to_enemy:** replace only the eight frontier first-step bits (725:733) with the enemy direction. Preserve all other fields, including original frontier presence and position.','- **target_and_route_to_enemy:** also set frontier presence, relative target coordinates and target distance to the adjacent enemy (distance one). Retain the original unknown-neighbor count and probe fields; these edits do not constitute a real frontier.','- **zero_frontier:** zero the entire 15-field frontier block (718:733) during adjacency.','- Outside adjacency, use the unchanged observation.','','These are deliberately synthetic observation interventions. Position-only inconsistency, nonexistent frontier targets and removal of expected inputs may put the model outside its training distribution. They test sensitivity to these fields, not a faithful observation redesign.','','## Same-state probabilities','','For every adjacent-combat state encountered by the unchanged sampled model, evaluate all four observations without changing the world or drawing another action. This avoids confusing input sensitivity with different rollout states. Values below describe '+str(r['fixed_state_comparison']['all_adjacent']['n'])+' decision-weighted states.','','| Input | Mean probability of any attack | Greedy would attack | Mean probability of selected enemy direction |','|---|---:|---:|---:|']
for name,m in r['fixed_state_comparison']['all_adjacent']['variants'].items():
 lines.append(f"| {name} | {100*m['mean_attack_probability']:.2f}% | {100*m['greedy_attack_fraction']:.2f}% | {100*m['mean_target_probability']:.2f}% |")
g=r['fixed_state_comparison']['original_greedy_nonattack'];lines+=['',f"Restricting to the {g['n']} states where the original highest-probability action was a non-attack:",'','| Input | Mean attack probability | Greedy would attack |','|---|---:|---:|']
for name,m in g['variants'].items():lines.append(f"| {name} | {100*m['mean_attack_probability']:.2f}% | {100*m['greedy_attack_fraction']:.2f}% |")
lines+=['','## Complete sampled rollouts','','| Input | Clears | Deaths | Timeouts | Mean physical turns |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for name,v in r['results'].items():
 m=v['metrics'];lines.append(f"| {name} | {m['clears']} | {m['deaths']} | {m['timeouts']} | {m['turns']:.1f} |")
lines+=['','## Interpretation','','Redirecting only the route bits increases mean attack probability on the identical states from 65.95% to 85.11%. Among originally greedy non-attack states, it changes the greedy choice to an attack in 62.29%. Changing target position/distance as well gives nearly the same same-state effect. This directly demonstrates sensitivity to the route cue, beyond the earlier observational alignment.','','Rollout gains are smaller: arrow redirection improves clears from 867 to 897, while zeroing the frontier yields 898. Zeroing actually lowers average attack probability across the original fixed states, despite increasing it in the originally non-attack subset. Thus the two interventions change different preferences, and neither rollout result can be explained solely by the overall mean attack probability. Full rollouts also visit different states.','','The cue can steer the model toward attacking, but redirecting it is not equivalent to a forced-attack rule and does not recover that rule’s full benefit. These experiments establish an input dependency, not that the exploration reward caused it or that synthetic cues are a robust fix.']
lines+=['','No attack/wait/retreat overrides are applied: every executed action is sampled from the model using the edited observation. The modified fields are copied into a separate tensor and never written to the simulator observation buffer. Sampled baseline outcomes, kills and physical turns exactly reproduce saved results. Every action passed the actual simulator mask and every episode terminated. Synthetic checks verify field boundaries, unchanged non-adjacent rows, no mutation of the original observation and correct eastward target/route encoding.','','Fixed-state changes directly measure the policy response to the input edits. Rollout comparisons are one model and a reused validation panel, and action changes also change combat RNG consumption. No general significance or transfer claim is made. These tests do not identify whether the exploration reward, training distribution or another factor caused the learned response.','','```sh','gpu-d1/.venv/bin/python gpu-d1/diagnostics/seek-destroy-los/frontier_enemy_probe.py','gpu-d1/.venv/bin/python gpu-d1/diagnostics/seek-destroy-los/report_frontier_enemy.py','```','','Raw results and provenance: `results/los-frontier-enemy-probe.json`.','']
(ROOT/'LOS-FRONTIER-ENEMY-PROBE.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines))
